@Steve L
On a slightly related note: the current fashion in France is to call anyone who is not a young girl "madame." Mademoiselle has fallen out of favor as sexist, which of course it is, although it's certainly still used by some folks.

Don't know about Spain. I have been called señorina, in my younger days, as a diminutive for a married woman. Now I would only expect señora.

@V
I heard it as:
A minister, a priest, and a rabbit walk into a blood bank. The rabbit says to the workers, “I think I’m a typo…”

@Jim 

They missed two. Ms is not a translation of señorita, or an abbreviation of Miss. 

Señorita and Miss are forms of address for and unmarried woman. Ms is a separate title explicitly created to give women a form of address that doesn't include their marital status, regardless of what it is.

@jp inframan

I don’t have a problem with it. Sometimes I capitalize my puns for that reason, people can be pretty dense sometimes. Although this one was pretty obvious and it does take a bit off the humor to advertise it.

I think the funnier thing is saying “no pun intended”, because most of the time you did intend it, or at least if it was an accident you could have reworded it if you didn’t want the pun there.

@Tony

I get what you mean. I suppose ultimately my complaint (of sorts) was about about differences between English in Polish. Over here "mieszanka warzyw" is specifically the mixed bag of raw, unseasoned vegetables, used to make a dish, main or side. It is never a dish itself. Even if the mixed vegetables have only been steamed or boiled, they would never feature as "mieszanka warzyw" on a menu: it would rather be "warzywa na parze" or "warzywa z wody" (steamed vegetables/vegetables out of the water, literally). It makes sense, right? The customer should be able to learn from the menu how the vegetables have been prepared.

My mother-in-law makes the weirdest salads BTW, so one day I may be served corn, beet, pea and kale 🤣

